A||||2||JohnnyK||Gas pedal cable.||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||11-27-2000||09:55 PM||||My gas pedal is very stiff, and the cable has a whole lot of cracks and rust around it in the back. First off, do I have to buy a Fiero specific cable, or can I just go to my local auto shop? Second, how is it routed? Under the car, console? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Gold-86SE||11-29-2000||11:05 PM||||<BR>About your statement "My gas pedal is very stiff..."  If the stiffness is down at the foot pedal, lubricate it with some WD-40, you may even try it in the engine bay - just be vary of starting unwanted fires.<P>Additionally about your statement, "My... cable has a whole lot of cracks and rust around it in the back."  If the throttle cable is frayed and rusty, it may be time to replace it.  Also, check the route of the cable, it may be binding on a part of the engine.<P>Your first question "do I have to buy a Fiero specific cable, or can I just go to my local auto shop?"  Thinking that you may have possible cost savings, I suggest you go for a Fiero specific throttle cable and save yourself some headaches.  You might get lucky and find one at a local parts yard.<P>As for the second question "How is it routed? Under the car, console?"  For the Duke the throttle cable is run through the fire wall behind the ECM under the console, which goes under the radio AC pod and down to the foot pedal.  V6's should be the same.  You will have to remove the center console and the radio AC pod to change the throttle cable.<BR><P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Cold and Rain Daily Driver)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||11-30-2000||01:11 PM||||I disconnect the cable from the TBI.. It's smooth through the cable, the actual pedal is smooth, and teh TBI works smooth. I guess it is supposed to feel that way..||||reg||1
